ash for Windows, as an MSIX package
===================================

Why this package needs a Python index at install time
-----------------------------------------------------

The package bundles ASH's own wheel and nothing else. The first time you run ash, the
launcher creates a virtualenv and installs that wheel into it, which resolves ASH's
27 runtime dependencies from whatever Python index the machine is configured for.

This is not an oversight. ASH's own code may ship in a package published by the
project; third-party code may not. Carrying exactly one wheel is the guard that keeps
this package on the right side of that line, because it can be checked by counting the
.whl entries in the package rather than by judging each dependency one at a time.

Building a self-contained offline package for your own machines is fine, and the
commands below are the supported way to do it. The line is about what the project
publishes, not about what the bytes are allowed to do.

On a machine with no reachable index, stage a wheelhouse first:

  pip download automated-security-helper==<version> -d C:\ash-wheels
  # then, before the first ash invocation:
  setx PIP_NO_INDEX true
  setx PIP_FIND_LINKS C:\ash-wheels

The package also does not carry a Python interpreter, so one in **[3.10, 3.14)** has to
be on the machine. The launcher tries `py -3.13`, `-3.12`, `-3.11`, `-3.10` in that
order and then falls back to `py -3`, `python3` and `python`, and checks each one's
version rather than trusting that it ran, because on a machine with no Python installed
a bare `python` resolves to the Microsoft Store placeholder that opens the Store
instead of running anything. ASH_MSIX_PYTHON overrides the search and is not
version-checked, so it can be pointed at 3.14 deliberately.

This bound is on WHICH INTERPRETER THE LAUNCHER PICKS. It is not ASH's `requires-python`,
which reads `>=3.10,<4` and is a different setting in a different file. The two are
independent and move for different reasons, and conflating them is easy enough that it
has already happened once: a bound here does not narrow what ASH supports, and widening
`requires-python` does not widen this.

The bound exists because `ash-unified-ci.yml` exercises 3.10 through 3.13 and nothing
above, so this package would otherwise pick an interpreter the MSIX verification has
never run against. `packaging/chocolatey` declares the same `python3 [3.10,3.14)` for the
same reason, and two Windows packages with one bound is deliberate.

When it is removed, and by what:

  1. main gains 3.14 in its CI matrices (PR #596, which also raises `requires-python`
     to `<3.15` -- that half is independent of this bound and does not on its own
     justify moving it)
  2. this branch merges that main, so the MSIX leg inherits 3.14 coverage
  3. the launcher bound rises to match, and Chocolatey's nuspec rises with it

Step 2 is the one that matters. Until the MSIX leg itself runs against 3.14, raising the
bound would ship a claim this package's own verification cannot check. The trigger is
written down because a bound whose removal condition is not recorded stops being a
decision and becomes folklore that nobody feels entitled to touch.

An earlier version of this file claimed more than that, and the correction is worth
recording. The bound was first introduced as a measured defect: on a `windows-latest`
runner the launcher's old `py -3` probe selected Python 3.14.7 and `ash --version`
exited 1 writing nothing to stdout or stderr, while the Chocolatey job passed on the
same image in the same run. That was read as "ASH is broken on Windows under 3.14". It
was not. With the bound in place the job pinned 3.13.15 and hit the identical failure;
the cause was this launcher building the virtualenv at a staging path and renaming it,
which invalidates every console-script shim inside it. Nothing is known to be wrong with
ASH on 3.14, and no claim to the contrary should be read out of this bound.

Why there is no install-time setup step
--------------------------------------

The .deb creates the virtualenv in a postinst scriptlet and the .rpm in %post. MSIX has
neither. A package is a signed, immutable directory that Windows extracts under
C:\Program Files\WindowsApps and never runs anything from at install time, so the venv
cannot exist when the package is installed and the only moment left to create it is the
first time you run ash. That is what the bundled launchers do, and it is why the first
invocation is slow and prints two lines to stderr saying so.

The venv could not be shipped inside the package even if there were somewhere to put it.
A virtualenv records the absolute paths and the interpreter ABI of the machine that
created it, so one built on a build host cannot be relocated to yours.

Where the venv goes, and what removes it
----------------------------------------

  %LOCALAPPDATA%\Packages\<PackageFamilyName>\LocalCache\ash-venv

That location is chosen for uninstall. MSIX has no uninstall hook either, so nothing can
delete the venv on the way out, but Windows does delete a package's own per-user state
directory when the package is removed. Putting the venv there is the only way to get the
behavior the .deb and .rpm have, where removing the package reclaims what pip installed
instead of leaving several hundred megabytes orphaned in a directory you would have to
know about.

The cost is roughly 60 characters of path, on top of a virtualenv's already deep
site-packages tree. On a machine without long paths enabled, a dependency with a deep
tree can exceed MAX_PATH while pip is installing. If that happens, point the venv
somewhere shorter:

  setx ASH_MSIX_VENV C:\ash-venv

A venv at a path you chose is not per-package state, so uninstall will not remove it.
That is the trade being made, stated so it is not a surprise later.

Two other files appear alongside it, and both are the launcher's.

  <venv>\.ash-bootstrap-complete   written after pip finishes
  <venv>.lock                      held while a first run is in progress

The launcher decides whether to bootstrap by looking for the marker, not by looking for
a console script, because pip writes the console scripts before the last of
site-packages and a bootstrap interrupted near the end would otherwise leave something
that looks finished. Deleting the marker is the supported way to force a rebuild on the
next `ash`; the run will remove what is there and install again. The lock is what stops
two shells running `ash` for the first time at the same moment from installing over each
other -- the second waits for the first and then finds the work done. If a run is killed
hard the lock file is left behind, which is harmless: it holds no lock once the process
is gone, and the next run reuses it.

The venv is built where it will live, and is never created elsewhere and moved. A
virtualenv on Windows cannot be relocated: pip writes each Scripts\*.exe with the
absolute path of the interpreter it was generated for baked in, so a moved venv has
shims naming a directory that no longer exists, while python.exe inside it keeps working
because it resolves its home through the relative pyvenv.cfg beside it. The failure that
produces is a console script exiting 1 with nothing on stdout or stderr.

Filesystem access, and the capability that is not the one doing the work
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

The manifest declares two restricted capabilities. They are not interchangeable and the
second one is the one people delete by mistake.

  runFullTrust            what actually lets ash read the tree you point it at
  broadFileSystemAccess   what puts ash in the Windows privacy settings page

A packaged app declared mediumIL, which this one is, runs with your own token and sees
the file system you see. That is runFullTrust. Remove it and ash installs, runs, and
scans nothing, because it is then an AppContainer app confined to its own storage.

broadFileSystemAccess is an AppContainer capability: it grants a sandboxed app the access
its user already has. On a mediumIL package it therefore grants nothing runFullTrust has
not already granted. It is declared anyway, and here is what it buys and what it costs.

What it buys. Windows lists apps that declare it under Settings > Privacy > File system,
with a toggle. A tool whose whole job is reading every file in a repository should be
visible and revocable where a user goes looking for exactly that, rather than invisible
there because it happens to be full trust. Microsoft's own guidance is to declare
privacy-sensitive capabilities even on full-trust packages for this reason.

Why a narrower grant cannot express what ASH needs. ASH's default source directory is the
process working directory, and its default output directory is `.ash\ash_output` inside
that same tree (automated_security_helper/cli/scan.py:634-640). So the directory ash must
read and write is not a fixed location a manifest could name: it is wherever you happened
to cd to, and it is different on every invocation. The narrower capabilities Windows
offers each name one fixed known folder, and two of them additionally require the manifest
to declare specific file extensions, which a scanner that must read every file in an
arbitrary repository cannot do. The mechanism Windows offers for arbitrary locations is a
file picker plus a future-access list, which is a GUI flow and not available to a command
that was handed a path on its command line.

What it costs. broadFileSystemAccess is a restricted capability. Sideloading a package
that declares one needs no approval from anybody, but submitting one to the Microsoft
Store requires written justification on the submission form explaining why the narrower
APIs are insufficient, and that submission can be refused. Nothing in this repository
submits to the Store, so today the cost is the toggle: a user who switches it off gets ash
terminated so Windows can apply the change.

This package is self-signed, and that has consequences
------------------------------------------------------

The package built by build.ps1 with no signing secret set is signed with a throwaway
self-signed certificate generated during the build. Two things follow.

It is not Store-installable. The Microsoft Store will not accept a self-signed package at
all, and nothing here submits to the Store or to any package registry.

It will not install anywhere until you decide to trust its certificate. That is the point
of the mechanism rather than an obstacle to it: trusting the certificate is you asserting
that you got this file from somewhere you trust.

To install it, in an elevated PowerShell:

  # 1. Pull the signing certificate out of the package you are about to install.
  #    Reading it from the package rather than downloading it separately means you are
  #    trusting the certificate that actually signed THIS file.
  $msix = 'C:\path\to\automated-security-helper-<version>.msix'
  $signature = Get-AuthenticodeSignature -FilePath $msix
  $signature.SignerCertificate | Format-List Subject, Thumbprint, NotAfter

  # 2. Look at that Subject and Thumbprint and decide. Then:
  [System.IO.File]::WriteAllBytes(
      "$env:TEMP\ash-signer.cer", $signature.SignerCertificate.RawData)
  Import-Certificate -FilePath "$env:TEMP\ash-signer.cer" `
      -CertStoreLocation 'Cert:\LocalMachine\TrustedPeople'

  # 3. Install.
  Add-AppxPackage -Path $msix

Trusted People, not Trusted Root Certification Authorities. The certificate is not a
certificate authority, and putting it in the root store would trust it to vouch for
anything else it signed, which is a much larger statement than "I want to run this one
program". It has to be the Local Machine store rather than the current user's, because
that is the only one the deployment service checks.

To remove the package and the venv it created:

  Get-AppxPackage -Name AWSLabs.AutomatedSecurityHelper | Remove-AppxPackage

And to withdraw the trust afterwards, which is worth doing if the certificate was a
throwaway one:

  Get-ChildItem Cert:\LocalMachine\TrustedPeople |
      Where-Object { $_.Subject -like '*ASH Development*' } | Remove-Item

Swapping in a real certificate
------------------------------

Set one secret. There is nothing else to change: no thumbprint, no certificate path, no
edit to AppxManifest.xml.

Windows refuses to install a package whose Identity/@Publisher is not byte-identical to
its signing certificate's subject, which is normally where this gets fiddly, because a
manifest with a hardcoded Publisher has to be hand edited the day the real certificate
arrives. build.ps1 instead reads the subject out of whichever .pfx it was given and stamps
that into the staged manifest. The self-signed path and the real-certificate path are the
same code with different bytes.

  MSIX_SIGNING_PFX_BASE64     base64 of a PKCS#12 file
  MSIX_SIGNING_PFX_PASSWORD   optional; a password-less .pfx keeps it to one secret

A real release also wants a timestamp, because an untimestamped signature stops validating
when the certificate expires. Pass -TimestampUrl to build.ps1 with your RFC 3161 endpoint.
It has no default on purpose: a default would put a particular timestamping service's URL
in this repository and would make every self-signed development build depend on reaching
it.

The three names, and why one of them is deliberately unfashionable
------------------------------------------------------------------

  ash                         canonical
  ashv3                       deprecated; warns once on stderr, then runs the same CLI
  automated-security-helper   kept indefinitely, and silent

The long name exists for Windows in particular. MSYS2 ships the Almquist shell as `ash`
and has already shadowed ASH's entry point, producing an "Illegal option" error from a
program nobody meant to run.

Which is worth knowing before you trust the short name here, because an app execution
alias is itself just a name on PATH. Windows resolves a collision between two apps
claiming the same alias by invoking whichever one registered it last, and a real
executable earlier on PATH wins over an alias outright. So installing this package does
not guarantee that typing `ash` reaches ASH. If an MSYS2 shell is on your PATH, use the
long name. That is what it is for.

You can also see and turn off each alias under Settings > Apps > Advanced app settings >
App execution aliases.

`-V` is `--version`. `-v` is `--verbose`. The launchers forward your command line
unchanged, so anything the CLI accepts works the same way here.

Scanners are not installed by this package
------------------------------------------

ASH drives third-party scanners; it does not contain them. After installing:

  ash dependencies install              # everything ASH can provision
  ash dependencies install --tool grype # or one at a time

The one exception is detect-secrets, which is a runtime dependency of ASH and drives in
process, so it works immediately. The rest are correctly reported SKIPPED rather than
MISSING until they are installed.

Version numbering
-----------------

MSIX requires quad notation: four dot-separated integers, major.minor.build.revision. ASH
uses three-part semver. The mapping is major.minor.patch.0, and the trailing zero is not
padding: the Microsoft Store reserves the fourth component and requires it to be 0 in a
submitted package, and ASH has no fourth component to put there.

A consequence worth knowing: a PEP 440 pre-release or local version, like 3.7.0rc1 or
3.7.0+local, has no representation in quad notation. build.ps1 refuses to package such a
wheel rather than dropping the suffix, because a package built from 3.7.0rc1 that called
itself 3.7.0.0 would install over the real release.

Building it yourself
--------------------

  # from a checkout, with a wheel already built by `uv build --out-dir dist`
  pwsh -File packaging/msix/build.ps1 -Wheel dist\automated_security_helper-<version>-py3-none-any.whl

  # or the whole thing, including installing it and running a real scan
  pwsh -File packaging/msix/verify-on-windows.ps1

build.ps1 needs makeappx.exe and signtool.exe, which ship with the Windows SDK and are not
on PATH by default; it looks for them under Windows Kits\10\bin and reports where it looked
if it cannot find them. If you have no SDK, `nuget install Microsoft.Windows.SDK.BuildTools`
carries both.

The manifest checks that do not need Windows at all run anywhere:

  uv run --python 3.13 --no-project python packaging/msix/msix.py validate

That is the same script build.ps1 runs, rather than a second copy of the same assertions.
It exists because AppxManifest.xml cannot be schema-validated off Windows: the appx
namespace URIs are not resolvable, and the only redistributable copy of the schema files is
missing 30 of the 47 namespaces its own schemas reference, including the one that declares
broadFileSystemAccess. `makeappx pack` applies the real schema, and that is the first point
at which it is applied.

Files in the package
--------------------

  AppxManifest.xml            identity, capabilities, and one Application per entry point
  ash.exe                     launcher; bootstraps the venv, then runs the venv's ash
  ashv3.exe                   the same launcher, compiled under a different name
  automated-security-helper.exe   likewise
  wheels\                     ASH's wheel, the package's only payload
  assets\                     the logo images the manifest is required to reference

The three launchers are one source file compiled three times. Each reads its own filename
to decide which venv console script to run, which is how `ashv3` ends up printing the
deprecation warning that lives in the wheel rather than a second copy of it written here.
